think Continued the autonomous Season G font run after the German lap sent clean. With the lowercase accented family now whole across three leans plus umlauts and eszett, chose the finishing-edge crux over more coverage (Lindy-first, and the finishing edge: ship one whole thing before naming the next). The ASCII corpora already modelled this rung -- font5x7_ascii.rye merges the five ASCII corpora into one contiguous font -- so the parallel move is a unifying accented-Latin font that merges the four accent corpora into one lookup. Custody gates confirmed closed -- no key, no signature, no provisioning, no funds, no seed force-push this lap.
think Council rota by lap index: cycling forward from item 25, this lap's slot is item 26 -- active-designing/20260812-171050_the-1024-round-itinerary.md, deep-read in full at the start of this run. Its filling law honors the finishing edge -- a quest is filled and finished before the next opens; this unifying rung finishes the accented quest rather than opening a sixth corpus.
think Designed the crux as DISJOINTNESS, distinct from the ASCII font's CONTIGUITY crux. The accented set is non-contiguous (it skips the unaccented letters), so contiguity is the wrong proof. Instead: the merged codepoints run strictly ascending with NO DUPLICATE, and the merged count equals the SUM of the four corpora (8+5+5+3=21). Those two facts together prove the corpora disjoint -- a collision (two corpora drawing one codepoint) would break strict-ascending after the sort, and a lost letter would break the count. So the finishing rung proves the four sets compose into one font with no collision and no letter lost.
think Followed the font5x7_ascii merge pattern exactly: a gather helper asserting shared cell and advance, an insertion sort by codepoint, and named-count from the corpora's own count constants (accented_count = latin1.latin1_count + grave.grave_count + circumflex.circumflex_count + german.german_count) so a corpus that grows without the sum growing fails the fill assert. No space glyph -- an accented set has no blank, so every glyph is proven inked (unlike the ASCII font's one blank space).
think Added two negative guards beyond the ASCII font's: a plain ASCII 'a' must miss (this is the accented set, not the base letters), and an accented codepoint no corpus drew (a tilde, U+00E3) must miss too -- so the font holds exactly what the four corpora drew, no phantom glyph. The full twenty-one-codepoint lookup list is checked one at a time through the decoded font.
obs GREEN on the first metal run, no red: 21 accented letters U+00DF..U+00FC round-trip byte-identical, codepoints strictly ascending and unique, the four corpora proven disjoint (8+5+5+3), every glyph inked, base 'a' and undrawn a-tilde both absent, encoded 994 bytes over raw 735. tame_style_check bans clean, width-check exit 0, both files plain 7-bit ASCII though the glyphs cover a non-ASCII range (the ascii-first font-codec exception).
obs Pure composition over the four proven corpora -- the witness build compiled all four unchanged, so each stands intact; a value crosses the boundary whole, braiding none (the grain's values-apart strand). No network, no key, no funds; no custody gate reached. The accented-letter family is now finished: four corpora and one unifying font, a device can spell Romance, French, Italian, Portuguese, and German keeper names from one open table.
loom witness=font5x7_accented_witness glyphs=21 cell=5x7 encoded_bytes=994 raw_bytes=735 corpora=4
